 - **Trying it locally:** run `python -m http.server` in `docs/` and open `/builder/`. (Opening `index.html` straight from disk works, but the icons below only load over http.)
-- **Look:** one font family (the system UI font) everywhere except the code box, and the colours are variables at the top of `style.css`: near-black panels, yellow-gold accent, a blood red, and a stripe of the seven Sin colours under the header. Change the palette there.
-- **Skills:** each skill has a live preview laid out like a skill on the Limbus wiki: the name banner, the coins, the damage type, `[On Use]`-style tags coloured by kind (blue Use, green Hit, orange Clash, violet Turn, gold skill-type tags), status and damage words coloured with their icon, and a numbered row per coin (a `:Coin2:` line lands on coin 2). `preview.js` makes it, and `tests/preview_check.js` plus `tests/test_builder_site.py` check it. "Add from an example" and "Copy" make a skill quicker; the examples live in `SKILL_EXAMPLES` in `game/builder_rules.py`. A row of links under the header jumps to each part of the form.
+- **Look:** one font family (the system UI font; the wiki's own skill-name font, Mikodacs, is not available) everywhere except the code box. The colours are variables at the top of `style.css` and are the ones the Limbus wiki uses, read from the inline styles of wiki pages: positive `#fac400`, negative `#e20000`, neutral `#9f6a3a`, text cream `#eecea4`, skill bar `#810000`, `[On Use]` `#26cfff`, every other timing green `#94f140`, `[Clash Win]` `#ff7e00`, `[Clash Lose]` `#e30000`. The whole page uses them (buttons, tabs, panels, tags), not only the skill preview. Each Sin has a dark and a light colour for the name banner and the frame ring (Envy is a guess: the sampled wiki pages had no Envy skill). A stripe of the seven Sin colours sits under the header. Change the palette in `:root`; `WikiLook` in `tests/test_builder_site.py` checks the values.
+- **Tabs:** every part of the form is its own tab: Identity, Your points, Stats, Resistances, Status ranks, Defense types, Group tags, Skills, Passives, Status effects. Click one, use the arrow keys, Home and End, or the Back and Next buttons under each part. The page remembers the tab you were on (browser session) and `#sec-skills` in the address opens that tab. A red dot on Skills or Passives means something there needs fixing. On a phone the summary card moves below the form (the bar at the bottom jumps to it).
+- **Skills:** each skill has a live preview laid out like a skill on the Limbus wiki: the name banner in the Sin's colours, the coins, the damage type, `[On Use]`-style tags coloured the wiki's way (blue On Use, orange Clash Win, red Clash Lose, green for every other timing, brown underlined skill-type tags), status words coloured by whether the effect is positive (yellow), negative (red) or neutral (brown) with their icon, and a numbered row per coin (a `:Coin2:` line lands on coin 2). `preview.js` makes it, and `tests/preview_check.js` plus `tests/test_builder_site.py` check it. "Add from an example" and "Copy" make a skill quicker; the examples live in `SKILL_EXAMPLES` in `game/builder_rules.py`.
 - **Rule lines are picked, not typed:** under each skill (and passive) a list shows its rule lines, and "Add a rule line" builds a new one in four steps: *When* it fires (all 43 timings, grouped, with a coin picker for the per-coin ones; passives only see the timings they react to), an optional *If* (18 conditions with their numbers, statuses and tags as inputs), *What it does* (30 effects: damage and power, statuses, Offense/Defense Level, Sanity, HP and Shield, survival, follow-ups) and *Extras* (aimed at an ally, wait until next turn, how often, chance). The page shows the finished line, or says why the choices do not fit (for example Deal +N% damage only works at Before Use, On Use, Clash Start and Before Attack). Typing the lines is still there under "Type or paste the lines as text instead". The choices come from `game/line_spec.py` (written into `rules.js`), the line is built by `docs/builder/lines.js`, and `tests/test_line_picker.py` feeds every combination to the bot's own parser both ways: a line the picker offers must parse, and a pair the picker refuses must be one the bot refuses. The skill tag buttons now include `[Unclashable]`, `[Indiscriminate]` and `[Unfocused Volley]`.
@@ -986,3 +987,3 @@ character: name, portrait link, stats, resistances, status ranks, defense types,
 - **Background:** `docs/builder/bg.jpg` is the page picture, already darkened and blurred (it is a 1600 px copy, so the page does not filter a big image while scrolling); a dark panel sits behind the form so text stays readable.
-- **Status list and colour key:** the last section of the page lists every status the builder offers (Sin, positive/negative/neutral, a short summary and a "How it works" fold-out) above a key to what the preview colours mean. The text is the bot's own `/help` status text from `game/help_content.py`, passed through `status_guide()` in `game/builder_rules.py` into `rules.js`, so the site and the bot cannot disagree; a test fails if a status the builder offers is missing from the list. Positive is yellow, negative red, neutral brown, as on the wiki.
+- **Status list and colour key:** the last section of the page lists every status the builder offers (Sin, positive/negative/neutral, a short summary and a "How it works" fold-out) above a key to what the preview colours mean. The text is the bot's own `/help` status text from `game/help_content.py`, passed through `status_guide()` in `game/builder_rules.py` into `rules.js`, so the site and the bot cannot disagree; a test fails if a status the builder offers is missing from the list. Positive is yellow, negative red, neutral brown, as on the wiki; Charge is the one neutral status in the list (a guess from earlier wiki reading, not seen on the sampled pages). The same key shows the resistance words in the wiki's colours: Fatal red, Weak pink, Normal cream, Endure grey, Ineffective dark grey, and each resistance field on the Resistances tab is tagged with its word.
 - **Icons:** the page can show the bot's own emoji next to statuses, HP and Speed. Run `python -m game.emoji_icons` once from a machine with internet access. It downloads every emoji in `game/emojis.py` into `docs/builder/icons/` and writes `icons/index.json`; commit that folder. Without it the page shows text only. Run it again after uploading new emoji. `tests/test_emoji_icons.py` covers the collecting and saving.
